            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Catalysts for the Rise of Indian Nationalism</h3>\n<p>Modern Indian nationalism was the collective historical product of internal social awakening and the contradictions inherent in British colonial exploitation:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Administrative and Economic Unification:</strong> The British created a single centralized state with uniform penal laws (IPC 1860, CrPC 1861), a unified currency, and all-India civil services. The construction of the <strong>railway network</strong>, the <strong>electric telegraph</strong>, and the <strong>uniform half-anna postal system</strong> physically compressed the subcontinent, enabling leaders from Lahore, Calcutta, Bombay, and Madras to consult, coordinate, and foster a pan-Indian identity.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Modern Intelligentsia &amp; European Thought:</strong> Western education introduced English as a common lingua franca among educated elites. Indian students devoured the democratic ideals of the American and French Revolutions and the Italian Risorgimento (Mazzini and Garibaldi), questioning why British constitutional liberties should be denied to India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Racial Arrogance:</strong> The blatant racial chauvinism of British administrators—visible in segregated railway carriages, clubs marked <em>'Dogs and Indians Not Allowed'</em>, and unequal court trials—deeply humiliated educated Indians, uniting them in national solidarity.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Evolution of Pre-Congress Political Associations</h3>\n<p>Before the founding of the Indian National Congress in 1885, political consciousness evolved through distinct regional associations:</p>\n\n<table class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Year</th>\n      <th>Association</th>\n      <th>Key Founders</th>\n      <th>Significance &amp; Focus</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1836</strong></td>\n      <td>Bangabhasha Prakasika Sabha</td>\n      <td>Associates of Ram Mohan Roy (Gaurishankar Tarkabagish)</td>\n      <td>First organized political association in India to discuss government policy.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1838</strong></td>\n      <td>Landholders' Society (Zamindari Association)</td>\n      <td>Dwarkanath Tagore, Radhakanta Deb, Prasanna Kumar Tagore</td>\n      <td>First organization to use constitutional agitation to protect class interests (zamindari rights).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1851</strong></td>\n      <td>British Indian Association</td>\n      <td>Radhakanta Deb (President), Debendranath Tagore (Secretary)</td>\n      <td>Amalgamated 1838 &amp; 1843 bodies; petitioned Parliament in 1852 for an Indian legislature.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1866</strong></td>\n      <td>East India Association (London)</td>\n      <td>Dadabhai Naoroji</td>\n      <td>Operated in London to lobby British MPs and public opinion on Indian welfare.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1870</strong></td>\n      <td>Poona Sarvajanik Sabha</td>\n      <td>M.G. Ranade, G.V. Joshi (Sarvajanik Kaka), S.H. Chiplunkar</td>\n      <td>Mediated between rural peasantry and government; spearheaded famine relief analysis.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1876</strong></td>\n      <td>The Indian Association of Calcutta</td>\n      <td>Surendranath Banerjea, Anandamohan Bose</td>\n      <td>First dynamic middle-class all-India platform; led the nationwide Civil Service Agitation (1877–78).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1884</strong></td>\n      <td>Madras Mahajana Sabha</td>\n      <td>M. Veeraraghavachariar, G. Subramania Iyer, P. Anandacharlu</td>\n      <td>United public opinion across the Madras Presidency.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1885</strong></td>\n      <td>Bombay Presidency Association</td>\n      <td>Pherozeshah Mehta, K.T. Telang, Badruddin Tyabji</td>\n      <td>Known as the 'Bombay Triumvirate', provided enlightened leadership to Western India.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
