            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Catalyst: Allan Octavian Hume</h3>\n<p>The definitive organizational breakthrough in modern Indian politics was catalyzed by <strong>Allan Octavian Hume</strong> (1829–1912), a retired British ICS officer and visionary administrator. In March 1883, Hume issued an impassioned circular letter to the graduates of Calcutta University, urging them to dedicate themselves to the service of the nation:</p>\n<blockquote>\n  <em>\"Whether in the individual or the nation, all vital progress must spring from within... If fifty men cannot be found with sufficient the power of self-sacrifice, sufficient love for and pride in their country, then there is no hope for India!\"</em>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Hume consulted with Lord Dufferin (the Viceroy), traveled to England to brief British MPs, and coordinated with prominent Indian leaders including Dadabhai Naoroji, Pherozeshah Mehta, and W.C. Bonnerjee.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The First Historic Session: Bombay (December 1885)</h3>\n<p>The inaugural session of the <strong>Indian National Union</strong> was initially scheduled to convene in <strong>Poona</strong> in late December 1885 under the auspices of the Poona Sarvajanik Sabha. However, just days before the meeting, a fatal outbreak of <strong>cholera</strong> erupted in Poona. The venue was hurriedly shifted to Bombay.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Date &amp; Hall:</strong> On <strong>28 December 1885</strong>, at 12 noon, the conference assembled at the <strong>Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College</strong>, located near Gowalia Tank in Bombay.</li>\n  <li><strong>The President:</strong> <strong>Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee</strong> (W.C. Bonnerjee), a leading barrister of the Calcutta High Court, was unanimously elected President upon the proposal of A.O. Hume and seconded by K.T. Telang.</li>\n  <li><strong>The 72 Immortal Delegates:</strong> The session was attended by exactly <strong>72 accredited delegates</strong> representing diverse regions, religions, and professions (lawyers, journalists, merchants, and teachers):\n    <ul>\n      <li>38 from Bombay Presidency (including Pherozeshah Mehta, Dinshaw Wacha, K.T. Telang).</li>\n      <li>21 from Madras Presidency (including G. Subramania Iyer, P. Anandacharlu).</li>\n      <li>3 from Bengal (W.C. Bonnerjee, Narendranath Sen, Girija Bhusan Mukherjee).</li>\n      <li>7 from North-Western Provinces and Awadh (Pandit Prannath, Babu Ganga Prasad Varma).</li>\n      <li>3 from Punjab (Lala Murlidhar of Ambala).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Name:</strong> On the motion of <strong>Dadabhai Naoroji</strong>, the name of the organization was changed from <em>Indian National Union</em> to the <strong>Indian National Congress</strong> (taking inspiration from the American legislative body to signify an assembly of the nation's people).</li>\n  <li><strong>A.O. Hume's Office:</strong> Hume was appointed the General Secretary of the Congress, an office he held with distinction for over two decades.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Four Primary Objectives Announced by W.C. Bonnerjee</h3>\n<p>In his presidential address, W.C. Bonnerjee enunciated the foundational charter of the Congress:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>To promote personal intimacy and friendship between earnest workers in the cause of India in different parts of the Empire.</li>\n  <li>The eradication, by direct friendly intercourse, of all possible race, creed, or provincial prejudices amongst all lovers of our country.</li>\n  <li>The authoritative recording of the matured opinions of the educated classes in India on some of the more important and pressing social and administrative questions of the day.</li>\n  <li>The determination of the lines upon, and methods by which, during the next twelve months, it is desirable for native politicians to labor in the public interest.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The session passed <strong>nine resolutions</strong> demanding: a Royal Commission on Indian administration, abolition of the Council of the Secretary of State, reform of legislative councils with elected Indian representation, reduction of military expenditure, and holding simultaneous ICS examinations in India and England.</p>\n",
