            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Titans of the Moderate Era</h3>\n<p>The early leadership of the Indian National Congress was composed of extraordinary intellectual and moral giants who laid the institutional foundations of modern Indian public life:</p>\n\n<h3>2. Profiles of Key Leaders</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Dadabhai Naoroji (1825–1917):</strong> Revered across the subcontinent as the <em>'Grand Old Man of India'</em> and the <em>'Patriarch of Indian Nationalism'</em>. In 1892, breaking centuries of racial barriers, Naoroji was elected as the <strong>first Asian/Indian Member of the British House of Commons</strong> representing Central Finsbury for Gladstone's Liberal Party. Standing on the floor of the British Parliament, he fearlessly exposed the poverty and economic drain of India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sir Pherozeshah Mehta (1845–1915):</strong> Celebrated as the <em>'Lion of Bombay'</em> (<em>Bombay no Sinh</em>) and the <em>'Uncrowned King of Bombay'</em>. A master jurist, Mehta drafted the Bombay Municipal Corporation Act of 1888 and established the <em>Central Bank of India</em> (first completely Indian-managed commercial bank) in 1911. He presided over the 1890 Calcutta Congress session, ruling the Moderate faction with an iron will.</li>\n  <li><strong>Surendranath Banerjea (1848–1932):</strong> Revered as <strong>Rashtraguru</strong> (Teacher of the Nation) and hailed as the <em>'Indian Burke'</em> for his roaring, thunderous English oratory that could hold crowds of 50,000 spellbound. Passed the prestigious ICS exam in London in 1869, but was dismissed by the racist British bureaucracy in 1874. Channeling his fury into politics, he founded the <em>Indian Association</em> (1876), edited <em>The Bengalee</em>, and authored his classic political autobiography <strong>A Nation in Making (1925)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Badruddin Tyabji (1844–1906):</strong> The first Indian barrister of Bombay High Court, Tyabji became the <strong>first Muslim President of the Indian National Congress</strong> at the 1887 Madras session. When communal reactionaries urged Muslims to stay away, Tyabji delivered an impassioned defense of secular unity, declaring: <em>'I do not believe that the Congress is a Hindu body... It is composed of all the diverse communities of India.'</em></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Gopal Krishna Gokhale: The Moral Saint of Politics</h3>\n<p>Born in Ratnagiri (Maharashtra), <strong>Gopal Krishna Gokhale</strong> (1866–1915) was the supreme intellectual star of the Moderate movement, whom Mahatma Gandhi acknowledged as his <strong>Rajya Guru (Political Guru)</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Servants of India Society (1905):</strong> On <strong>12 June 1905</strong> in Pune, Gokhale founded the <em>Servants of India Society</em>. Its members (including Srinivas Sastri, Amritlal Thakkar / Thakkar Bapa, and N.M. Joshi) took vows of pure selfless service, poverty, and constitutional discipline to organize education, relief during disasters, and tribal welfare.</li>\n  <li><strong>Parliamentary Statesmanship:</strong> On the Imperial Legislative Council (1902–1915), Gokhale was peerless. He delivered masterly annual budget analyses that exposed the crushing burden of the Salt Tax, the wasteful military expenditures, and proposed a historic bill in 1911 for <strong>free and compulsory primary education</strong> across India.</li>\n</ol>\n",
