            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Four Pillars of Assertive Nationalism</h3>\n<p>The Extremist leaders discarded the Moderate policy of 'praying and petitioning'. In its place, they formulated a militant, self-reliant <strong>Fourfold Program</strong>:</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Fourfold Program Explained</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Swaraj (Self-Rule):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>To the Moderates, self-government meant partial autonomy within the British Empire over a distant period.</li>\n      <li>To the Extremists, <strong>Swaraj was an immediate, non-negotiable birthright</strong>. Tilak defined Swaraj as complete domestic self-governance. <strong>Sri Aurobindo Ghosh</strong> and <strong>Bipin Chandra Pal</strong> went further, defining Swaraj as <strong>absolute, unconditional national independence</strong> free from all foreign control. Aurobindo wrote: <em>'Political freedom is the life-breath of a nation; to attempt social reform, educational expansion, or industrial development without political freedom is the very height of human folly.'</em></li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Swadeshi (Indigenous Production):</strong> Swadeshi was the positive economic gospel of national self-reliance. It meant consuming only goods produced in India, reviving domestic handloom weaving, and founding Indian-owned cotton mills, match factories, banks, and steamship lines (such as <strong>V.O. Chidambaram Pillai's</strong> <em>Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company</em> in Tuticorin).</li>\n  <li><strong>Boycott (Economic and Social):</strong> Boycott was the negative, aggressive weapon. It aimed at inflicting direct commercial damage on British industrial capitalists by refusing to buy British cloth, sugar, and Liverpool salt. It expanded into a total <strong>Social Boycott</strong>—priests refused to solemnize marriages where the bride wore foreign garments, and washermen refused to wash foreign-made clothes.</li>\n  <li><strong>National Education:</strong> Breaking the colonial monopoly on education by establishing schools and colleges managed by Indian national councils, teaching through the mother tongue, and combining vocational technical training with patriotic history and physical culture.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Sri Aurobindo's 'Doctrine of Passive Resistance' (1907)</h3>\n<p>In April 1907, <strong>Sri Aurobindo Ghosh</strong> published a series of historic articles in the daily paper <em>Bande Mataram</em>, providing the definitive philosophical and tactical manual of <strong>Passive Resistance</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Premise:</strong> British rule in India was physically impossible without the administrative, judicial, and economic cooperation of Indians. The moment Indians withdrew that cooperation, the colonial state would paralyze:\n    <blockquote><em>\"The primary method of passive resistance is the refusal of cooperation... We refuse to pay taxes, we refuse to send our children to your schools, we refuse to take our disputes to your courts, and we refuse to consume your commodities.\"</em></blockquote>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Defensive Non-Violence:</strong> Aurobindo argued that passive resistance should remain non-violent so long as the colonial state acted within the law; but if the government resorted to brutal physical terror, Indians possessed the legitimate moral right to meet force with force in self-defense.</li>\n</ul>\n",
