                "Concept of Swaraj: Unlike the Moderates who sought Dominion Status under the British Crown, Extremists demanded Swaraj—defined by Tilak as complete autonomy and self-rule, and by Aurobindo and B.C. Pal as absolute national independence with zero foreign control.",
                "Boycott: The most formidable economic and political weapon; aimed at striking directly at the financial pockets of British industrialists by refusing to purchase British manufactured goods (Manchester cloth, Liverpool salt).",
                "Swadeshi: The positive constructive counterpart to boycott; promoting indigenous handloom weaving, Indian-owned banks, insurance companies, soap factories, and shipping lines (V.O. Chidambaram Pillai's Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company).",
                "National Education: Creating an independent national educational system outside British government control to instill national pride, physical courage, and scientific enterprise.",
                "Passive Resistance: Formulated systematically by Sri Aurobindo Ghosh in a series of articles in 'Bande Mataram' in April 1907 (The Doctrine of Passive Resistance).",
                "Aurobindo's Passive Resistance: Refusal to pay taxes if necessary, total non-cooperation with colonial administration, boycott of British law courts and schools, while maintaining non-violence unless attacked by force.",
                "Mass Mobilization: Extremists transformed the freedom movement from an upper-class debating club into a mass movement embracing students, women, lower-middle classes, and urban workers."
