            "title": "Repressive Measures of the Colonial Government (Seditious Meetings Act, Criminal Law Amendment) & Split at Surat (1907)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Colonial State's Reign of Repression</h3>\n<p>Terrified by the revolutionary mass awakening in Bengal, Maharashtra, and Punjab, the colonial administration of Viceroy <strong>Lord Minto II</strong> (1905–1910) and Home Member Sir Harold Stuart abandoned all pretenses of liberal governance, unleashing a barrage of draconian legislation:</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Arsenal of Repressive Laws (1907–1910)</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Carlyle Circular (October 1905):</strong> Issued by Bengal Chief Secretary R.W. Carlyle, threatening to withdraw government grants, scholarships, and university affiliations from any school or college whose students participated in Swadeshi picketing or chanted <em>Vande Mataram</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Barisal Outrage (April 1906):</strong> At the Bengal Provincial Conference in Barisal, British police superintendent Kemp led armed constables to violently assault peaceful delegates with lathis, chasing young men into ponds and severely injuring Chittaranjan Guha Thakurta for chanting <em>Vande Mataram</em>, while Surendranath Banerjea was arrested and fined.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention of Seditious Meetings Act (1907):</strong> Prohibited public political gatherings of more than twenty persons in designated proclaimed areas without prior police permission.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Explosive Substances Act (1908):</strong> Introduced capital punishment or life transportation for possessing or manufacturing explosives, following the rise of revolutionary bomb societies.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Newspapers (Incitement to Offences) Act (1908):</strong> Empowered magistrates to summarily forfeit and seize printing presses of anti-British publications, extinguishing radical journals like <em>Yugantar</em>, <em>Sandhya</em>, and <em>Bande Mataram</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Indian Press Act of 1910:</strong> Re-imposed heavy security deposits (up to Rs. 5,000) on all newspapers, leading to the forfeiture of nearly 1,000 presses over the next decade.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Deportation Without Trial &amp; The Divide-and-Rule Trap</h3>\n<p>Resurrecting the archaic <strong>Bengal Regulation III of 1818</strong>, the colonial state bypassed ordinary courts and deported top leaders to distant penal fortresses without charges, trial, or legal defense:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In May 1907, <strong>Lala Lajpat Rai</strong> and <strong>Sardar Ajit Singh</strong> were arrested and deported to <strong>Mandalay Jail in Burma</strong>.</li>\n  <li>In December 1908, nine eminent Bengal leaders—including <strong>Ashwini Kumar Dutt</strong>, <strong>Krishna Kumar Mitra</strong>, and Sachindra Prasad Bose—were deported without trial.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Simultaneously, Secretary of State <strong>John Morley</strong> and Viceroy <strong>Lord Minto</strong> pursued a calculated strategy: <strong>'Rally the Moderates'</strong>. They dangled the carrot of constitutional council reforms before Pherozeshah Mehta and G.K. Gokhale, while wielding the brutal stick of police terror against Tilak and Aurobindo. Anxious to protect their constitutional status, the Moderates became increasingly fearful of the Extremists, setting the stage for the disastrous showdown at Surat in December 1907.</p>\n",
