            "title": "Foundation of the All-India Muslim League (1906, Dacca): Nawab Salimullah, Aga Khan & British Communal Strategy",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Imperial Divide-and-Rule &amp; The Simla Deputation</h3>\n<p>The phenomenal mass mobilization of the Swadeshi Movement in 1905 alarmed the British colonial administration. Determined to erect a permanent communal wall between Hindus and Muslims, colonial officials encouraged conservative Muslim aristocrats to organize an independent political front loyal to the Crown:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The 'Command Performance' (Simla, 1 October 1906):</strong> Guided by <strong>W.A.J. Archbold</strong> (Principal of MAO College, Aligarh) and colonial civil servants, a hand-picked delegation of 35 prominent Muslim nobles, jagirdars, and lawyers led by <strong>Aga Khan III</strong> (Sultan Muhammad Shah) met Viceroy <strong>Lord Minto</strong> at Simla.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Demands:</strong> The delegation submitted an address demanding:\n    <ol>\n      <li><strong>Separate Communal Electorates:</strong> Muslim representatives in legislative councils should be elected exclusively by Muslim voters.</li>\n      <li><strong>Weightage:</strong> Muslim representation should be in excess of their numerical proportion in the population, in recognition of their political importance and military services to the Empire.</li>\n      <li>A separate, dedicated Muslim university (upgrading MAO College).</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Imperial Embrace:</strong> Lord Minto accepted their demands without reservation. That evening, an official wrote to Lady Minto, who recorded in her diary:\n    <blockquote><em>\"A work of statesmanship has been accomplished today which will affect India and our Empire for many a long day. It is nothing less than the pulling back of sixty-two millions of people from joining the ranks of the seditious opposition.\"</em></blockquote>\n    Maulana Mohammad Ali later aptly termed the Simla Deputation a <em>'Command Performance'</em> scripted and directed from Whitehall.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Birth of the All-India Muslim League (Dacca, 1906)</h3>\n<p>Following the triumph at Simla, delegates gathered in <strong>Dacca</strong> for the annual <em>All-India Muhammadan Educational Conference</em>. On <strong>30 December 1906</strong>, at the invitation of <strong>Nawab Salimullah of Dacca</strong>, a political session was convened at Shahbagh under the presidency of <strong>Nawab Viqar-ul-Mulk</strong> (Mushtaq Hussain):</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Resolution:</strong> Nawab Salimullah moved the resolution to establish the <strong>All-India Muslim League</strong>, seconded by Hakim Ajmal Khan and Maulana Zafar Ali Khan.</li>\n  <li><strong>Leadership &amp; Headquarters:</strong> <strong>Aga Khan III</strong> was elected as the permanent President of the League (a post he held until 1913), with <strong>Nawab Mohsin-ul-Mulk</strong> and <strong>Nawab Viqar-ul-Mulk</strong> appointed as joint secretaries. The central office of the League was established at <strong>Lucknow</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Declared Objectives:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>To foster among Indian Muslims feelings of steadfast loyalty to the British Crown.</li>\n      <li>To protect and advance the political rights and interests of the Muslim community through respectful representations.</li>\n      <li>To prevent the rise of prejudice or hostility between Muslims and other communities without prejudice to the other objects of the League.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p>The first regular annual session was held at <strong>Karachi in December 1907</strong>, and the constitution (the <em>Green Book</em> drafted by Maulana Mohammad Ali) was formally adopted. In its early years, the League steadfastly supported the Partition of Bengal and opposed Congress boycotts, functioning as a loyalist conservative body until progressive younger leaders (like M.A. Jinnah and the Ali brothers) transformed it during the First World War.</p>\n",
