            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Delhi Durbar of 1911: The Royal Concessions</h3>\n<p>In December 1911, the British government staged the grandest imperial spectacle in Asian history—the <strong>Delhi Durbar of 1911</strong>—to celebrate the coronation of <strong>King George V and Queen Mary</strong> (the only reigning British Emperor and Empress ever to set foot on Indian soil):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Annulment of the Partition of Bengal:</strong> On <strong>12 December 1911</strong>, King George V made the dramatic royal proclamation annulling Lord Curzon's hated 1905 Partition of Bengal. Bengal was reunited as a single province on linguistic lines. The annulment stood as a towering, historic triumph for the Swadeshi Movement.</li>\n  <li><strong>Birth of Bihar Province:</strong> Concurrently, non-Bengali linguistic regions were severed: <strong>Bihar and Orissa were separated from Bengal to form a new province</strong> (taking administrative effect on <strong>1 April 1912</strong>, celebrated as <em>Bihar Divas</em>), while Assam was restored as a Chief Commissioner's province.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shifting the Imperial Capital to Delhi:</strong> The King announced the immediate transfer of the capital of the British Indian Empire from <strong>Calcutta to Delhi</strong>. Calcutta had become too dangerous and politically volatile, infested with underground bomb societies. Delhi, with its centuries of Mughal and sultanate history, provided imperial prestige and a central geographic location. British architects Sir Edwin Lutyens and Sir Herbert Baker were commissioned to design New Delhi.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Delhi Conspiracy Case (23 December 1912)</h3>\n<p>The British administration believed that shifting the capital to Delhi would insulate the government from revolutionary terror. They were in for a terrifying shock:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The State Entry:</strong> On <strong>23 December 1912</strong>, Viceroy <strong>Lord Hardinge II</strong> made his grand ceremonial state entry into the new capital of Delhi, seated atop a richly caparisoned elephant in a royal procession through the historic bazaar of <strong>Chandni Chowk</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Bomb Attack:</strong> Just as the viceregal elephant reached near the Punjab National Bank building in Chandni Chowk, a young revolutionary named <strong>Basant Kumar Biswas</strong> (dressed in a woman's veil), acting on the instructions of revolutionary mastermind <strong>Rash Behari Bose</strong> and <strong>Sachindra Nath Sanyal</strong>, threw a powerful bomb directly into the viceregal carriage.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Aftermath:</strong> The royal umbrella-bearer (Mahabir Prasad) was blown to pieces, and Lord Hardinge was severely wounded in the back and neck with shrapnel, fainting in a pool of blood. Lady Hardinge escaped unhurt. The procession halted in terror.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Trials &amp; Bose's Masterly Escape:</strong> Following an extensive CID investigation, the <em>Delhi Conspiracy Case</em> trial was launched:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Four patriots—<strong>Master Amir Chand</strong>, <strong>Avadh Behari</strong>, <strong>Bhai Bal Mukund</strong>, and <strong>Basant Kumar Biswas</strong>—were sentenced to death and hanged in May 1915.</li>\n      <li>The mastermind, <strong>Rash Behari Bose</strong>, who had slipped back to Dehradun and even organized a public meeting to condemn the attack to deflect suspicion, evaded capture. In May 1915, disguised as a relative of Rabindranath Tagore named <em>P.N. Tagore</em>, he slipped aboard the Japanese vessel <em>Sanuki Maru</em> and escaped to Japan, where he established the Indian Independence League and organized the INA before handing it over to Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n",
